How to find unique PFAMS/CAZymes in each genome compared to the others:
Since you were manipulating the original CAZ/PFAM summaries, obtain those summaries again but put them into a new dataframe
```{r}
#Here is the one for PFAMs
dniv_pfam <- dniv$PFAMs 
heterobasidion_pfam <- heterobasidion$PFAMs
stereaceae_pfam <- stereaceae$PFAMs
```
```{r}
#Here is the one for CAZymes
dniv_cazymes <- dniv$CAZy
heterobasidion_cazymes <- heterobasidion$CAZy
stereaceae_cazymes <- stereaceae$CAZy
```

Then you want to use the setdiff feature to detect what is unique in your genome of interest and write it to a new dataframe
```{r}
#Here is the one for PFAMS
dniv_unique_pfams <- setdiff(dniv_pfam, union(heterobasidion_pfam, stereaceae_pfam))
heterobasidion_unique_pfams <- setdiff(heterobasidion_pfam, union(dniv_pfam, stereaceae_pfam))
stereaceae_unique_pfams <- setdiff(stereaceae_pfam, union(dniv_pfam, heterobasidion_pfam))
```
```{r}
#Here is the one for CAZymes
dniv_unique_cazy <- setdiff(dniv_cazymes, union(heterobasidion_cazymes, stereaceae_cazymes))
heterobasidion_unique_cazy <- setdiff(heterobasidion_cazymes, union(dniv_cazymes, stereaceae_cazymes))
stereaceae_unique_cazy <- setdiff(stereaceae_cazymes, union(dniv_cazymes, heterobasidion_cazymes))
```

You can summarize the unique PFAM and CAZy data with the following 
```{r}
length(dniv_unique_pfams)
length(dniv_unique_cazy)
length(heterobasidion_unique_pfams)  
length(heterobasidion_unique_cazy)
length(stereaceae_unique_pfams)
length(stereaceae_unique_cazy)
```
